# core.vendor — what RIDES ALONG in a vendored core/, beyond the Core payload itself.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The vendored set is `core.manifest` ∪ THIS FILE. scripts/sync-core.sh materializes
# exactly that subset into each OS repo's core/; everything else in this repo stays
# in this repo.
#
# THE TWO LISTS ANSWER DIFFERENT QUESTIONS. Keep them straight:
#
#   core.manifest — is this SHIPPED Core? Authored once here, vendored, and symlinked
#                   into $HOME by an OS repo's bootstrap. The user-facing config.
#   core.vendor   — does this RIDE ALONG inside core/ because an OS repo's Makefile,
#                   CI or bootstrap reads it from there? Never symlinked; it exists so
#                   that vendored tooling resolves.
#
# A path in BOTH is a FAIL, not a warning (audit-core.sh §1d): an entry in both means
# nobody decided which it is. A tracked file in NEITHER is repo-meta that stays here, and
# lives in audit-core.sh's META_ALLOWLIST / META_PREFIXES.
#
# Those three are NOT a strict partition, and it is worth knowing why. META_ALLOWLIST is
# exact paths and does not overlap this file — §1d fails it if it ever does. META_PREFIXES
# is `scripts/`, `.github/`, `examples/` and friends, which DO contain the handful of files
# listed below; enumerating the non-vendored remainder per-file instead would be ~90 lines
# churning every time a dev script lands, to state the same thing. So the prefixes are the
# residual, the overlap is intended, and the gate checks the drift that actually happens:
# someone adds a consumer here and forgets to delete the old exact allowlist entry.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. CONTRIBUTING.md claimed for years that repo-meta and dev
# tooling were "not vendored into OS repos". It was false: the allowlist only kept
# them out of core.manifest, which governs SYMLINKING, not what lands on disk. The
# subtree copied the whole tree regardless — 5.6 MB into each of nine repos, of which
# ~0.9 MB was Core, and the single largest file was a 1.8 MB README GIF no OS repo
# displays. This file makes the claim true (#676).
#
# READ FROM THE PINNED COMMIT, NEVER FROM A WORKTREE. scripts/lib/core-vendor.sh
# resolves this file as `${sha}:core.vendor`. That is load-bearing: sync-core.sh builds
# the filtered tree inside the CONSUMER and core-integrity.sh rebuilds it inside CORE,
# and the two must agree by construction. Reading either side's working tree would let
# the producer and the verifier compute different trees — the #556 failure, reintroduced.
#
# THE PRESENCE OF THIS FILE AT A COMMIT IS THE VERSION SWITCH. A Core commit that
# carries core.vendor vendors the FILTERED tree; one that does not predates filtering
# and vendored its whole tree. core_lock_expected_tree() derives which from the pinned
# commit, so repos still pinning an older Core stay `pristine` with no flag day.
#
# GRANULARITY: per-file for scripts/, .github/ and examples/. A `scripts/` directory
# entry would drag in release-only tooling nothing on a box runs, and would make the
# closure check (§1e) yield nothing — the same argument core.manifest makes for listing
# zsh/completions/ per-file (B10, #454). Trailing `/` for a genuine directory unit is
# supported by the format, but prefer naming files.
#
# EVERY ENTRY NAMES ITS CONSUMER. If you cannot name one, it does not belong here —
# that is the whole point of the file. Four of the entries below are load-bearing via
# HINT STRINGS or YAML rather than a `source` line, which the §1e closure walker cannot
# see; they are called out individually for exactly that reason.
#
# `# entry` marks a BFS ROOT for audit-core.sh §1e's transitive-closure check: a file a
# consumer EXECUTES or SOURCES directly. Everything those roots reach must also be
# listed here or in core.manifest, or the audit fails.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── the two contracts themselves ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Vendored tooling reads these from its OWN root, so a filtered core/ that omitted them
# would break the moment anything in it parsed the manifest. They also let a checkout
# with no Core objects answer "why are these files here?" from core/ alone.
core.manifest
core.vendor

# ── root-level data ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
gitleaks.toml          # secret-scan config: MacBook Makefile:92, Arch:117, Alpine:78 (+ .pre-commit-config.yaml:68), Gentoo:101, openSUSE secrets.yml:96, Offense Makefile:120-121 + checks.yml:109,115
PORTING-MATRIX.md      # named by the CORE PAYLOAD in a hint string (zsh/30-functions.zsh:747) and by Debian packages.yml:236, Arch bootstrap.sh:280, and ~20 os/*.capabilities headers — invisible to §1e, hence listed by hand

# ── scripts/ — the four things an OS repo actually runs from core/ ────────────
# Everything else under scripts/ is AUTHORING apparatus (release, fan-out, benchmarks,
# dashboards) with no consumer on a box or in an OS repo, and stays out.
scripts/tool-versions.env       # pinned tool versions: MacBook ci.yml:115,136,138,315,348,361,363 + Makefile:119, Offense checks.yml:72,127,130 + claude-routines.yml + Makefile:21, Defense Makefile:28 + tests/test-defense.sh:865 + tests/lint-shell.sh:41
scripts/check-capabilities.sh   # entry — os.capabilities linter run by SEVEN repos: Fedora Makefile:122, Arch:159, Debian:186, openSUSE:162, Alpine:124, Gentoo:117, MacBook:241. Standalone (sources nothing).
scripts/core-integrity.sh       # entry — the per-repo `--self` tamper gate; MacBook test/verify-core.sh delegates to it rather than re-implementing the tree comparison (#593's move, applied again)
scripts/lib/common.sh           # entry — sourced directly by MacBook test/check-return-traps.sh:49,68 for _core_return_trap_hits; also the base lib for the above
scripts/lib/core-lock.sh        # reached from core-integrity.sh
scripts/lib/core-vendor.sh      # reached from core-lock.sh — the filtered-tree builder itself
scripts/os-repos.txt            # reached from common.sh :: load_os_repos (fleet mode)

# ── .github/ — only what a consumer's workflow resolves LOCALLY ──────────────
# Most `uses:` in the fleet are REMOTE refs (dotgibson/dotfiles-core/.github/...@vN) and
# need nothing vendored. These two are the exceptions: a `./core/...` local path, and a
# workflow that Defense reads as a FILE rather than calling.
.github/actions/setup-core-tools/action.yml  # `uses: ./core/.github/actions/setup-core-tools` — MacBook ci.yml:141,366; Offense checks.yml:84
.github/workflows/lint-call.yml              # read as a FILE, not called: Defense tests/test-defense.sh:871 greps SHELLCHECK_OPTS out of it, tests/lint-shell.sh:42

# ── examples/ — templates a bootstrap installs or a hint string names ────────
examples/atuin-daemon.service     # installed at bootstrap: Fedora bootstrap.sh:379, Debian bootstrap.sh:684
examples/os.capabilities.example  # named by the CORE PAYLOAD in hint strings (zsh/02-capabilities.zsh:114, zsh/55-maint.zsh:488,528, zsh/60-update.zsh:762) and by seven OS Makefiles — invisible to §1e, hence listed by hand
